# Git handoff

The orchestrating agent prepares this handoff. Other agents do not create branches, stage, or commit. Fast Development Mode is unchanged: no extra agents or review stages. A few lines of execution feedback in the final report are not a new stage.

## Before implementation

Inspect the current branch, `git status`, and existing branch names. Do not overwrite, discard, stage, commit, stash, or restore unrelated changes. Do not stage secrets or gitignored env files.

## When to prepare it

After the verification that task already requires has passed, propose the handoff. Do not commit during implementation.

- Keep the current branch when it already fits the work and is not `main`, `master`, or `develop`.
- Otherwise propose one branch, `feature/<short-name>` or `fix/<short-name>`. If the repo already uses a branch pattern, follow that pattern.
- Do not commit on `main`, `master`, or `develop` unless the user explicitly authorizes that branch.
- Creating a branch must leave unrelated uncommitted files unstaged. Do not move, stash, or clean them.
- Push, force-push, rebase, reset, cherry-pick, merge, and pull requests need separate explicit authorization.

## Approval

Show the proposal and wait. A general request to finish with a commit does not approve a specific branch, file list, or message. Use Cursor's native Git confirmation when it is offered; otherwise ask in chat.

- Current branch
- Proposed branch, if it differs
- Files to stage
- Suggested commit message, in the repo's existing style
- Brief summary
- Checks passed or failed
- Unrelated changes left untouched

After approval, run only those commands. Stop if the branch, status, or diff no longer matches the proposal. With no approval, stop here and leave the work uncommitted.

Report a commit only after it succeeds, with the branch name and short SHA.
